## Sodium chloride (table salt)
component-sodium-chloride-electrolysis-note = Under current salt water decomposes: hydrogen forms at the cathode, chlorine at the anode, and caustic soda (NaOH) stays in the solution: 2NaCl + 2H₂O → Cl₂ + H₂ + 2NaOH.
component-sodium-chloride = Sodium chloride (table salt)
    .genitive = table salt
component-sodium-chloride-description-novice = Sodium chloride (NaCl) — ordinary salt. It dissolves well in water: up to ≈ 36 g in 100 g of water. Any more stays on the bottom as crystals.
component-sodium-chloride-description-school = The solubility of NaCl barely depends on temperature: ≈ 35.7 g per 100 g of water at 0 °C and ≈ 39 g at 100 °C. So salt is obtained by evaporation rather than by cooling. In water salt splits into Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions — that is why salt water conducts electricity.
